```html
<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <!-- Introduction Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Introduction
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      Welcome! This section will introduce you to the concept of uniform acceleration, a fundamental concept in physics. We'll explore what it means for an object to accelerate uniformly and how to calculate this acceleration. Understanding uniform acceleration is key to understanding motion.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Definition Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Definition
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      Uniform acceleration means that an object's velocity changes by the same amount during each equal time interval. In simpler terms, the object speeds up or slows down at a constant rate. This constant rate of change in velocity is what we call uniform acceleration.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Key Concepts Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Key Concepts
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Velocity:</b> The rate of change of an object's position with respect to time. It has both magnitude (speed) and direction.</li>
        <li><b>Acceleration:</b> The rate of change of an object's velocity with respect to time.</li>
        <li><b>Uniform:</b> Constant or unchanging. In this context, it means the acceleration remains the same throughout the motion.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Formula Card -->
  <div class="formula-card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Main Formula
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <div class="formula">
        $$a = \frac{v_f - v_i}{t}$$
      </div>
      <div class="formula-explanation">
        Where:
        <ul>
          <li>\(a\) = acceleration</li>
          <li>\(v_f\) = final velocity</li>
          <li>\(v_i\) = initial velocity</li>
          <li>\(t\) = time taken</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-buttons">
        <button class="btn btn-primary">Explain Formula</button>
        <button class="btn btn-secondary">Open Formula Lab</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Applications Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Applications
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      Uniform acceleration is observed in various real-world scenarios:
      <ul>
        <li><b>Free Fall:</b> Objects falling under the influence of gravity (neglecting air resistance) experience uniform acceleration.</li>
        <li><b>Motion on Inclined Planes:</b> Objects sliding down a frictionless incline experience constant acceleration.</li>
        <li><b>Vehicles:</b> A car accelerating at a constant rate.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Advantages Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Advantages
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Simplified Calculations:</b> Makes calculations easier using kinematic equations.</li>
        <li><b>Predictability:</b> Allows us to predict the position and velocity of an object at any given time.</li>
        <li><b>Foundation:</b> Provides a fundamental understanding of motion for more complex scenarios.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Disadvantages Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Disadvantages
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Idealization:</b> Often assumes negligible air resistance or friction, which may not always be realistic.</li>
        <li><b>Limited Scope:</b> Does not apply to situations where acceleration is not constant.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Important Notes Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Important Notes
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ul>
        <li>Acceleration is a vector quantity; it has both magnitude and direction.</li>
        <li>The units of acceleration are typically meters per second squared (m/s²).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Summary Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Summary
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      Uniform acceleration is constant change in velocity over time. The formula \(a = \frac{v_f - v_i}{t}\) can be used to calculate the acceleration. Understanding this concept is crucial for understanding the motion of objects.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Suggested Questions Card -->
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-header">
      Suggested Questions
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ul>
        <li>What is the difference between speed and velocity?</li>
        <li>How does air resistance affect acceleration?</li>
        <li>Can you provide an example of non-uniform acceleration?</li>
        <li>Solve a problem. An object initially at rest accelerates at 2 m/s² for 5 seconds. What is its final velocity?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

